new file mode 100644
index 0000000..cd9824a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/developing/terrain-lod.md
@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
+# Terrain level of detail (LOD)
+
+Luanti's client can build a coarse, opaque terrain surface alongside each regular mapblock mesh. At the configured distance (`terrain_lod_start_distance`, in nodes), the solid render pass selects the coarse mesh instead of that mapblock's full-detail solid mesh. The default switch is enabled; set `terrain_lod_enabled = false` to keep full-detail solid meshes at every distance.
+
+The first implementation is deliberately conservative. It samples globally aligned 4-node horizontal cells, uses a median surface height, and reuses Luanti's tile, lighting, material, fog, and mesh-buffer paths. Only opaque, non-animated `NDT_NORMAL` nodes with unit visual scale enter the LOD. Liquids, transparent geometry, foliage, nodeboxes, and other special drawtypes remain in the ordinary mesh; transparent geometry is still rendered and sorted by the existing path. The LOD is opaque-only and is not used for the shadow-map pass.
+
+This is a native Luanti terrain-LOD foundation, not a drop-in port of Minecraft's Distant Horizons. It does not generate, persist, or request map data outside the blocks already received from the server, so it cannot display an unseen horizon. The simplified surface also intentionally loses caves, overhangs, buildings and fine terrain detail at distance. The regular mesh remains the source for nearby rendering, transparency and mapblock occlusion metadata.
+
+The main tuning control is the switch distance. The LOD grid stride is currently a conservative implementation constant (4 nodes); it should become a quality setting only after seam behavior, cache invalidation and performance have been measured across mapblock sizes and negative world coordinates.
